      # Accessibility Statement

      This accessibility statement applies to the View Court Data online service (abbreviated to VCD).

      This website is run by the Legal Aid Agency (LAA). We want as many people as possible to be able to use this website. For example, that means you should be able to:

      - navigate VCD using speech recognition software
      - navigate VCD using a keyboard
      - change colours, contrast levels and fonts using browser or device settings
      - listen to most of the website using a screen reader (including the most recent versions of JAWS, NVDA and VoiceOver) - see exceptions below

      We've also made the website text as simple as possible to understand.

      [AbilityNet](https://mcmw.abilitynet.org.uk/) has advice on making your device easier to use if you have a disability.

      ## How accessible this website is

      We know some parts of this website are not fully accessible:

      - On one page, there is an area not currently being read out by screen reader software.
      - Some values are not being read out in an optimum way by screen reader software.
      - Some headers are not currently tagged correctly in the code.
      - Some URLs and page titles are currently inconsistent or apply to multiple pages.
      - There are some validation issues in the code.
      - Some content needs to be reworded so that it is read out in a more concise manner by a screen reader. This is not a Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) failure, but is notable enough to mention.
      - On the 'Manage users' page, you have to navigate back to the top of the filter component to apply filters after selecting their options. This is not a Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) failure, but is notable enough to mention.

      ## Reporting accessibility problems with this website

      We're always looking to improve the accessibility of this website. If you find any problems not listed on this page or think we're not meeting accessibility requirements, contact [mojprod@service-now.com](mailto:mojprod@service-now.com) on Teams or via email.

      ## Enforcement procedure

      The Equality and Human Rights Commission (EHRC) is responsible for enforcing the Public Sector Bodies (Websites and Mobile Applications) (No. 2) Accessibility Regulations 2018 (the 'accessibility regulations'). If you're not happy with how we respond to your complaint, [contact the Equality Advisory and Support Service (EASS)](https://www.equalityadvisoryservice.com/).

      ## Technical information about this website's accessibility

      The Legal Aid Agency (LAA) is committed to making its website accessible, in accordance with the Public Sector Bodies (Websites and Mobile Applications) (No. 2) Accessibility Regulations 2018.

      ## Compliance status

      The website has been tested against the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.2 AA standard.

      This website is partially compliant with the [Web Content Accessibility Guidelines version 2.2](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/) AA standard, due to the non-compliances listed below.

      ## Non-accessible content

      The content listed below is non-accessible for the following reason(s):

      - On one page, there is an area that is not currently being read out by screen reader software: the content about the respondent on the 'Breaches' overview page. This fails WCAG 2.2 success criterion 1.3.2 (meaningful sequence). We plan to fix this issue so that all content is read out by a screen reader, and will test all new pages with a screen reader in future.
      - Some headers are not currently tagged correctly in the code (as &lt;h1&gt;, &lt;h2&gt;, &lt;h3&gt;, and so on), so they are less likely to be announced or mapped properly by assistive software. This fails WCAG 2.2 success criterion 2.4.10 (section headings). We plan to check our code and fix anything that isn't currently tagged correctly.
      - Some URLs and page titles are currently inconsistent or apply to multiple pages, which can confuse assistive technology, create incorrect bookmarks, and increase cognitive load for users. This fails WCAG 2.2 success criterion 2.4.2 (page titled). We plan to check our code and fix anything that isn't currently indexed correctly.

      ## What we're doing to improve accessibility

      Our [accessibility audit report](https://dsdmoj.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/ACD2/pages/6043436411/Accessibility+Audit+-+Actions+Fixes+Progress+Report) shows how and when we plan to improve accessibility on this website, with an outline of journeys both before and after changes have been made.

      ## Preparation of this accessibility statement

      This statement was prepared on 17th February 2026. It was last reviewed on 23rd June 2026. The aim is that it will be reviewed quarterly.

      This website was last tested on 15th January 2026 against the WCAG 2.2 AA standard.

      The test was carried out by the LAA. The most viewed pages were tested using automated and manual testing tools internally. A further audit of the website was carried out to the WCAG 2.2 AA standard.
